  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...An area of enhanced low-mid level convergence \nalong a frontal zone will interact with moderate instability and\na plume of rich, deep, anomalous moisture, supporting scattered\nto numerous showers and thunderstorms with rainfall rates of \n1-3\"/hr. Convection is not expected to be all that deep, and may\nnot contain much lightning, but PWATs above the 90th percentile\nfor early October and slow storm motions will pose a risk of \nsome hydro concerns, including at least a modest risk of a few \ninstances of flash flooding. The highest rainfall rates, and \ngreatest risk of hydro impacts, looks to be focused along and \nsouthwest of a Greenville to Morehead City line. It's this area \nwhere the highest instability will overlap with the greatest \nforcing and lift. Ensemble guidance continue to suggest 1-3\" of \nrain, on average, in this area. Where convection is the most \npersistent/trains, the potential exists for amounts as high as \n3-6\". Across the northeastern counties of ENC, and the OBX, \nweaker instability should limit rainfall rates and amounts, with\na much lower flooding risk.\n\nConvection may wane some tonight, although there should still \nbe sufficient lift and elevated instability to keep a risk of \nshowers and thunderstorms going through the night. On Sunday, a \nweak area of low pressure will ride along the stalled frontal \nzone and will continue to support periods of moderate to heavy \nrain and a few thunderstorms. Instability should take a step \ndown tomorrow, and rainfall rates may not get quite as high. \nHowever, given the anomalous moisture present, there may \ncontinue to be some hydro impacts, especially for areas that see\nrepeated rounds of heavier rain on top of what falls today. The\nheaviest rain is expected to end from north to south late \nSunday into Monday morning. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...In the wake of the weekend front, a stiff \nNEerly breeze will develop and peak MON night into TUE. This \nwill lead to building seas and elevated water levels. \nAstronomical tide levels will luckily be on the way down, but \nseas building to 7ft, though relatively choppy periodicity of 7 \nsec, may be enough to lead to further beach and dune erosion \nconcerns across the areas with severely diminished dune \nstructures. However, this risk appears to be decreasing some due\nto the strongest winds and highest seas shifting south of\nHatteras Island. Strongest winds of 20-25 kt with higher gusts \nexpected MON night into TUE morning. Winds remain out of the \nN-NE into late- week, but speeds diminish to 10-15kt late TUE \nand beyond.",
